 [17592186435806 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435806 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435806 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435806 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{y-x}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435806 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04c36299-59f0-54ea-b2d4-6a2a60540beb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435806 #edb/ref 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<\\frac{1}{y-x}<1 \\\\ y-x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1,\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435807 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7303070a-5c11-542d-9464-8fd77a6c4c78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435807 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- $\\sqrt{x+y}$ is defined for $x$ and $y,$ such that $x+y\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡g(x,y)$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}$ is defined for values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy $x+y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435808 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435808 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435808 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435808 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{xy}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435808 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75318d12-00c4-544e-97a9-126ab4836587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435808 #edb/ref 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nxy\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,xy\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435809 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435809 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435809 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435809 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435809 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8d614db-1f17-5013-b1ed-45363a374722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435809 #edb/ref 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435810 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $z=\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}$ at the point $(3,-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb2bfb86-4e76-5321-aed7-06eaaf8188f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435810 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,-\\pi /3)} & =\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,{|}_{(3,-\\pi /3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
